      "text": "To determine the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we should first consider all possible outcomes for each die. There are 6 faces on each die, so the first die has 6 possible outcomes and the second die also has 6 possible outcomes. This means that there are \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible combinations when rolling the two dice.\n\nNext, we need to identify the successful outcomes. A number less than three on the first die corresponds to rolling 1 or 2 on the first die. Similarly, a number greater than three on the second die corresponds to rolling 4, 5, or 6 on the second die. Thus, the successful outcomes are:\n(1, 4), (1, 5), (1, 6), (2, 4), (2, 5), (2, 6).\n\nSo, there are 6 successful outcomes out of the 36 possible combinations.\n\nTherefore, the probability is equal to the fraction of successful outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{Number of successful outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{6}{36}.\n\\]\nNow, we simplify this fraction:\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{36} = \\frac{1}{6}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
