      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan rolls a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to calculate the individual probabilities of each event and then find the combined probability.\n\nFirst, we calculate the probability that Nathan rolls a number less than three on the first die. A six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. There are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes. So, the probability for the first die is:\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the probability that Nathan rolls a number greater than three on the second die. The numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6. There are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes. So, the probability for the second die is:\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the two events (rolling a number less than three on the first die and rolling a number greater than three on the second die) are independent, we can find the combined probability by multiplying the individual probabilities:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1 \\times 1}{3 \\times 2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
